# ruff: noqa: E402 — env vars must be set before numpy/pymc imports

"""Posited model (what gets FIT to data generated by generate_data.py).

    y_ig ~ N(s(x_ig) + mu_g, sigma^2)
    mu_g ~ N(mu0, tau^2)
    mu0  ~ N(0, 1)
    lam  ~ Exp(5)          # smoothing (regularization) parameter for s(x)
    sigma, tau ~ Exp(1)

s(x) is a penalized regression spline of basis dimension k (the "spline
basis dimension" from the paper -- e.g. 6, 12, 24, 48). Penalization is
implemented the standard P-spline way: the k spline coefficients follow a
1st-order random walk whose innovation SD is governed by lam. Larger lam
-> smaller innovation variance -> smoother/more constrained fit. This is
the conventional way "lambda ~ Exp(5)" cashes out as an actual prior on
spline coefficients in a Bayesian GAM.

IDENTIFIABILITY FIX (sum-to-zero constraint on s(x)):
Previously, mu0 (the population mean of the mu_g's) was confounded with
the overall vertical level of s(x) -- nothing pinned down "where the
spline's baseline sits" vs. "what the average group offset is". The
standard remedy (used in mgcv and most Bayesian GAM implementations) is to
constrain the smooth term to average zero over the observed data:

    sum_i s(x_i) = 0

This is implemented below by centering the deterministic spline output at
every posterior draw: s_x_centered = s_x - mean(s_x). That removes the
smooth term's one shared degree of freedom in the vertical direction,
leaving mu0 (and the mu_g's) as the sole carriers of overall level --
the standard GAM identifiability constraint, with no change needed to the
random-walk penalty structure on beta.

NOTE: k is a property of this fitted model only, never of the DGP
(see generate_data.py, which has no k parameter at all).
"""

